{"name":"Cannabis / CBD for sleep","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":2,"casual":{"lead":["Some people use cannabis or cannabidiol (CBD) to fall asleep more easily, aiming to wake up rested, recover better from training and look fresher around the skin and eyes.","The chain runs through faster sleep onset and more recovery. Wikipedia's CBD article says clinical evidence for a sleep benefit is limited and lists sleepiness as a common effect; only small trials exist, such as a 14-person phase 2 study of cannabinoids for insomnia in depression. CBD is the non-intoxicating constituent, while THC is the main intoxicating one."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People take CBD as oils, gummies or capsules in the evening, or use cannabis products before bed, usually as part of a wind-down routine. Users describe the effect as a sense of calm and sleepiness within the hour; how long until sleep quality noticeably changes varies by person."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["The FDA has cited possible liver toxicity, drug interactions, effects on mood and alertness, and unknown long-term effects. FDA studies found many CBD products were inaccurately labeled, and about half of 147 tested contained THC. Insomnia is listed among adverse effects of the approved CBD drug Epidiolex, which is indicated for seizure disorders rather than sleep. Legal status varies widely by country and state."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/Cannabis_crop_growing_in_the_Seychelles.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/Cannabis_crop_growing_in_the_Seychelles.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Cannabis crop growing in the Seychelles","license":"CC BY 4.0","creator":"Sharon Uranie","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cannabis_crop_growing_in_t